  "worked_solution": "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$",
